helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3153770				0	lv	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Numeric expression that specifies an angle in radians that you want to calculate the cosine for.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3145749				0	lv	To convert degrees to radians, multiply degrees by pi/180. To convert radians to degrees, multiply radians by 180/pi.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664				0	lv	degree=(radian*180)/pi				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3146985				0	lv	radian=(degree*pi)/180				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3152885				0	lv	Pi is here the fixed circle constant with the rounded value 3.14159...				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153951				0	lv	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3155855				0	lv	REM The following example allows for a right-angled triangle the input of				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03080102.xhp	0	help	par_id3149484				0	lv	REM secant and angle (in degrees) and calculates the length of the hypotenuse:				20200411 15:09:53
