helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Translation of Controls in the Dialog Editor				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	bm_id8915372				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>dialogs;translating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>localizing dialogs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>translating dialogs\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	hd_id3574896				0	lv	\<variable id=\"translation\"\>\<link href=\"text/sbasic/guide/translation.xhp\"\>Translation of Controls in the Dialog Editor\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id4601940				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>The Language toolbar in the Basic IDE dialog editor shows controls to enable and manage localizable dialogs.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id9538560				0	lv	By default, any dialog that you create only contains string resources for one language. You may want to create dialogs that automatically show localized strings according to the user's language settings.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id6998809				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Select the language for the strings that you want to edit. Click the Manage Languages icon to add languages.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id71413				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Click a language, then click Default to set the language as default, or click Delete to remove the language from the list.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id2924283				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Opens a dialog where you can add a language to the list.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id5781731				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Select a language in the list and click Delete to remove that language. When you remove all languages, the string resources for localizable dialogs are removed from all dialogs in the current library.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id6942045				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Select a language in the list and click Default to set the language as default language.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id4721823				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>The default language will be used as a source for all other language strings.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id5806756				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Add UI languages for your dialog strings.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	hd_id6596881				0	lv	To enable localizable dialogs				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id8750572				0	lv	In the Basic IDE dialog editor, open the Language toolbar choosing \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>View - Toolbars - Language\</item\>. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id2224494				0	lv	If the current library already contains a localizable dialog, the Language toolbar is shown automatically.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id7359233				0	lv	Click the \<emph\>Manage Languages\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id2526017\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_managelanguage.png\" width=\"0.1665inch\" height=\"0.1665inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id2526017\"\>Manage Language icon\</alt\>\</image\> on the Language toolbar or on the Toolbox bar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id6549272				0	lv	You see the Manage User Interface Language dialog. The dialog manages languages for the current library. The name of the current library is shown on the title bar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id6529740				0	lv	Click Add in the dialog to add a language entry. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id7811822				0	lv	This step enables all new dialogs to contain localizable string resources.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id9121982				0	lv	The first time you click Add, you see the Set Default User Interface Language dialog. The following times you click Add, this dialog has the name Add User Interface Language.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id3640247				0	lv	You can also change the default language in the Manage User Interface Language dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id3808404				0	lv	Select a language. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id4585100				0	lv	This adds string resources to contain the translated versions of all strings to the dialog properties. The set of dialog strings of the default language is copied to the new set of strings. Later, you can switch to the new language and then translate the strings.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id2394482				0	lv	Close the dialog or add additional languages.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	hd_id631733				0	lv	To edit localizable controls in your dialog				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id2334665				0	lv	Once you have added the resources for localizable strings in your dialogs, you can select the current language from the Current Language listbox on the Language toolbar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id8956572				0	lv	Switch the Current Language listbox to display the default language.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id500808				0	lv	Insert any number of controls to your dialog and enter all strings you want.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id8366649				0	lv	Select another language in the Current Language listbox.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id476393				0	lv	Using the control's property dialogs, edit all strings to the other language.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id2655720				0	lv	Repeat for all languages that you added.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id3682058				0	lv	The user of your dialog will see the strings of the user interface language of the user's version of %PRODUCTNAME, if you did provide strings in that language. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id5977965				0	lv	If no language matches the user's version, the user will see the default language strings. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\translation.xhp	0	help	par_id3050325				0	lv	If the user has an older version of %PRODUCTNAME that does not know localizable string resources for Basic dialogs, the user will see the default language strings.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Opening a Dialog With Program Code				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154140				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>module/dialog toggle\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>dialogs;using program code to show (example)\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>examples; showing a dialog using program code\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154140				0	lv	\<variable id=\"show_dialog\"\>\<link href=\"text/sbasic/guide/show_dialog.xhp\" name=\"Opening a Dialog With Program Code\"\>Opening a Dialog With Program Code\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3145171				0	lv	In the \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> BASIC window for a dialog that you created, leave the dialog editor by clicking the name tab of the Module that the dialog is assigned to. The name tab is at the bottom of the window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3153968				0	lv	Enter the following code for a subroutine called \<emph\>Dialog1Show\</emph\>. In this example, the name of the dialog that you created is "Dialog1":				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3156443				0	lv	Sub Dialog1Show				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3148575				0	lv	BasicLibraries.LoadLibrary("Tools")				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3152463				0	lv	oDialog1 = \<link href=\"text/sbasic/guide/sample_code.xhp\" name=\"LoadDialog\"\>LoadDialog\</link\>("Standard", "Dialog1")				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3148646				0	lv	oDialog1.Execute()				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3147349				0	lv	End Sub				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3152596				0	lv	Without using "LoadDialog" you can call the code as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710				0	lv	Sub Dialog1Show				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3146985				0	lv	DialogLibraries.LoadLibrary( "Standard" )				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3155418				0	lv	oDialog1 = CreateUnoDialog( DialogLibraries.Standard.Dialog1 )				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3154944				0	lv	oDialog1.Execute()				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3145800				0	lv	End Sub				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3153157				0	lv	When you execute this code, "Dialog1" opens. To close the dialog, click the close button (x) on its title bar.				20200411 15:09:53
