helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\migrate_macros.xhp	0	help	hd_id0112200902353472				0	lv	\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/migrate_macros.xhp\"\>Migrate Macros\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\migrate_macros.xhp	0	help	par_id0112200902353466				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>The Database Document Macro Migration Wizard moves existing macros from sub-documents of an old Base file into the new Base file's macro storage area.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\migrate_macros.xhp	0	help	par_id0224200911454780				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Choose a location and file name to save the new database file. By default, the new file gets the same name as the old file, while the old file gets renamed with the string "backup" in the name.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\migrate_macros.xhp	0	help	par_id022420091145472				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>The list shows all changes that were applied to the database file.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\migrate_macros.xhp	0	help	par_id0112200902353542				0	lv	Previously, macros have been allowed to reside only in the text sub-documents of forms and reports. Now macros can also be stored in the Base file itself. This means that macros in Base files can be called now from any of its sub-components: forms, reports, table design, query design, relation design, table data view.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\migrate_macros.xhp	0	help	par_id0112200903075865				0	lv	However, it is technically not possible to store macros both in a Base file and in its sub-documents at the same time. So, if you want to attach some new macros to the Base file, while retaining any existing old macros that were stored in the sub-documents, you must move the existing old macros up to the Base file's macro storage area. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\migrate_macros.xhp	0	help	par_id0112200903075830				0	lv	The Database Document Macro Migration Wizard can move the macros up into the Base file's storage area. You can then examine the macros and edit them as needed. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\migrate_macros.xhp	0	help	par_id0112200903075951				0	lv	For example, it is possible that macros from the sub-documents had the same module names and macro names. After you moved the macros into one common macro storage area, you must edit the macros to make the names unique. The wizard cannot do this.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\migrate_macros.xhp	0	help	par_id0112200903075915				0	lv	The wizard can backup the Base file to another folder of your choice. The wizard changes the original Base file. The backup remains unchanged.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\migrate_macros.xhp	0	help	par_id0112200902353554				0	lv	\<link href=\"https://wiki.openoffice.org/wiki/Macros_in_Database_Documents\"\>An in depth explanation by the developers (Wiki).\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
