helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Inserting Brackets				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153415				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>brackets; inserting in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;brackets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>distances between brackets\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153415				0	lv	\<variable id=\"parentheses\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/parentheses.xhp\" name=\"Inserting Brackets\"\>Inserting Brackets\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150751				0	lv	In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Math, can brackets be shown separately so that the distance between them is freely definable?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3083281				0	lv	You can set individual brackets using "left" and "right", but the distance between the brackets will not be fixed, as they adapt to the argument. Nevertheless, there is a way to display brackets so that the distance between them is fixed. To accomplish this, place a "\\" (backslash) before the normal brackets. These brackets now behave like any other symbol and the alignment is the same as with other symbols:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3155960				0	lv	left lbrace x right none				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3148489				0	lv	size *2 langle x rangle				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3150344				0	lv	size *2 \\langle x \\rangle				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Working with Limits				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	bm_id8404492				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>limits;in sums/integrals\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>integral limits\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	hd_id1892147				0	lv	\<variable id=\"limits\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/limits.xhp\"\>Working with Limits\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	hd_id9881893				0	lv	How can I define the limits in a Sum or Integral formula?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id6504409				0	lv	You want to insert a summation formula like "summation of s^k from k = 0 to n" at the cursor in a Writer text document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8811304				0	lv	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Object - Formula\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id1276589				0	lv	You see the Math input window and the Elements window. If you don't see the Elements window, you can enable it in the View menu.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3283791				0	lv	In the upper part of the Elements window, click the \<emph\>Operators\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9734794				0	lv	In the lower part of the Elements window, click the \<emph\>Sum\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9641712				0	lv	To enable lower and upper limits, click additionally the \<emph\>Upper and Lower Limits\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3304239				0	lv	In the input window, the first placeholder or marker is selected, and you can start to enter the lower limit: 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8679158				0	lv	k = 0				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8471327				0	lv	Press F4 to advance to the next marker, and enter the upper limit:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id4731894				0	lv	n				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id759300				0	lv	Press F4 to advance to the next marker, and enter the summand:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id651875				0	lv	s^k				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id6756187				0	lv	Now the formula is complete. Click into your text document outside the formula to leave the formula editor.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9406414				0	lv	In the same way, you can enter an Integral formula with limits. When you click an icon from the Elements window, the assigned text command is inserted in the input window. If you know the text commands, you can enter the commands directly in the input window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id5866267				0	lv	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Object - Formula\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id1965697				0	lv	Click in the input window and enter the following line:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9004486				0	lv	int from{a} to{b} f(x)`dx				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id4651020				0	lv	A small gap exists between f(x) and dx, which you can also enter using the Elements window: click the \<emph\>Format\</emph\> icon, then the \<emph\>Small Gap\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3877071				0	lv	If you don't like the font of the letters f and x, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Fonts\</item\> and select other fonts. Click the \<emph\>Default\</emph\> button to use the new fonts as default from now on.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3021332				0	lv	If you need the formula within a line of text, the limits increase the line height. You can choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Text Mode\</item\> to place the limits besides the Sum or Integral symbol, which reduces the line height.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id260322				0	lv	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03090909.xhp\"\>Example of Integral and Sum ranges\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Merging Formula Parts in Brackets				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152596				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>brackets; merging formula parts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formula parts; merging\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>fractions in formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>merging;formula parts\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152596				0	lv	\<variable id=\"brackets\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/brackets.xhp\" name=\"Merging Formula Parts in Brackets\"\>Merging Formula Parts in Brackets\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154511				0	lv	Inserting fractions into formulas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3146971				0	lv	In the case of a fraction whose numerator and denominator consist of a product, a sum, and so on, the values that belong together must be bracketed together.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3149021				0	lv	Use the following syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3083280				0	lv	{a + c} over 2 = m				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3154703				0	lv	or				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3150297				0	lv	m = {a + c} over 2				20200411 15:09:53
