helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\main.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147341				0	lv	\<variable id=\"main\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/main.xhp\" name=\"Instructions for Using $[officename] Math\"\>Instructions for Using $[officename] Math\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\main.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150199				0	lv	Entering and Editing Formulas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Entering Line Breaks				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	bm_id1295205				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>line breaks; in formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas;line breaks\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>wrapping text;in formulas\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146970				0	lv	\<variable id=\"newline\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/newline.xhp\" name=\"Entering Line Breaks\"\>Entering Line Breaks\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147339				0	lv	How to write formulas in $[officename] Math over two lines (with manual line break):				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	par_id3154702				0	lv	Create a line break by using the "newline" command. Everything coming after the line break is placed on the next line.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Entering Comments				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155961				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>comments; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;comments in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155961				0	lv	\<variable id=\"comment\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/comment.xhp\" name=\"Entering Comments\"\>Entering Comments\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154657				0	lv	How does one attach comments that don't appear in the document to a formula?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_id3149499				0	lv	A comment begins with a double percent sign \<emph\>%%\</emph\>, and extends to the next line-end character (Enter key). Everything that lies in between is ignored and is not printed out. If there are percent signs in the text, they are treated as part of the text.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D0				0	lv	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D3				0	lv	a^2+b^2=c^2 %% Pythagorean theorem.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Manually Aligning Formula Parts				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156384				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>aligning; characters in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formula parts; manually aligning\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156384				0	lv	\<variable id=\"align\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/align.xhp\" name=\"Manually Aligning Formula Parts\"\>Manually Aligning Formula Parts\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154657				0	lv	How do you align characters in $[officename] Math quickly and easily?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3150249				0	lv	To accomplish this, you must define empty groups and character strings. They do not require any space, but carry information that helps in the alignment process.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153912				0	lv	To create empty groups, enter curly brackets \<emph\>{}\</emph\> in the Commands window. In the following example, the goal is to achieve a line break so that the plus signs are beneath one another, even though one less character is entered in the upper line:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153246				0	lv	a+a+a+{} newline {}{}{}{}{}a+a+a+a				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3143229				0	lv	Empty character strings are a simple way to ensure that texts and formulas are left-aligned. They are defined using double inverted commas "" . Make sure you do not use any typographic inverted commas. Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153809				0	lv	"A further example." newline a+b newline ""c-d				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149018				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>accessibility; $[officename] Math shortcuts\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149018				0	lv	\<variable id=\"keyboard\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/keyboard.xhp\" name=\"Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)\"\>Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150298				0	lv	You can control $[officename] Math without a mouse.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150343				0	lv	Inserting a Formula Directly				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659				0	lv	If you want to insert a formula into a text document, and you already know the correct writing, you can proceed as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153818				0	lv	Write the formula into your text				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153915				0	lv	Select the formula				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150213				0	lv	Choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154767				0	lv	Inserting a Formula using a Window				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3149875				0	lv	If you want to use the $[officename] Math interface to edit a formula, choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\> without any text selected.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391				0	lv	The cursor waits in the Commands window and you can type the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150537				0	lv	You can compose formulas using the Elements window. Open it with the menu \<emph\>View - Elements\</emph\> if it is not already open.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3155625				0	lv	If the Elements window is open, use F6 to switch from the Commands window to the Elements window and back.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154554				0	lv	Elements window				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Changing Default Attributes				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145792				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>attributes; changing in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>font attributes;changing defaults\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting;changing default attributes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defaults;changing default formatting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>changing;default formatting\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145792				0	lv	\<variable id=\"attributes\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/attributes.xhp\" name=\"Changing Default Attributes\"\>Changing Default Attributes\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154484				0	lv	Can default formats in $[officename] Math be modified?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3148870				0	lv	Some parts of formulas are always formatted bold or italic by default.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3150210				0	lv	You can remove these attributes using "nbold" and "nitalic". Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3143231				0	lv	a + b				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810				0	lv	nitalic a + bold b.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3149872				0	lv	In the second formula, the a is not italic. The b is bold. You cannot change the plus sign by this method.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Entering Text				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155962				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>text strings; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>direct text; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;text in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id5676442				0	lv	\<variable id=\"text\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/text.xhp\" name=\"Entering Text\"\>Entering Text\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id8509170				0	lv	How to enter direct text strings that do not get interpreted?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id337229				0	lv	Some text strings get interpreted as operators automatically. Sometimes this is not what you want. If you want to write W\<emph\>*\</emph\> (a letter with a superscripted asterisk), the asterisk will be interpreted as a multiplication operator. Enclose the direct text within double quotes or add spaceholders.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D0				0	lv	Examples:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id521866				0	lv	An imported MathType formula contains the following string				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9413739				0	lv	W rSup { size 8{*} }				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id755943				0	lv	If you have set up Math to convert imported MathType formulas (in \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - Load/Save - Microsoft Office), you see the formula with a placeholder instead of the asterisk.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id5988952				0	lv	Change {*} to {} * {} as in the following formula:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id3304544				0	lv	W rSup { size 8{} * {} }				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id4941557				0	lv	You can also use W^"*" to enter the character as direct text.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9961851				0	lv	Some formulas start with an = sign. Use "=" to enter that character as direct text.				20200411 15:09:53
