helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Using Scenarios				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149664				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>scenarios; creating/editing/deleting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>opening;scenarios\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>selecting;scenarios in Navigator\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	hd_id3125863				0	lv	\<variable id=\"scenario\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/scenario.xhp\" name=\"Using Scenarios\"\>Using Scenarios\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3150869				0	lv	A $[officename] Calc scenario is a set of cell values that can be used within your calculations. You assign a name to every scenario on your sheet. Define several scenarios on the same sheet, each with some different values in the cells. Then you can easily switch the sets of cell values by their name and immediately observe the results. Scenarios are a tool to test out "what-if" questions.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149255				0	lv	Creating Your Own Scenarios				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3154704	16			0	lv	To create a scenario, select all the cells that provide the data for the scenario. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3154020				0	lv	Select the cells that contain the values that will change between scenarios. To select multiple cells, hold down the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>\</item\>key as you click each cell.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3150364				0	lv	Choose \<emph\>Tools - Scenarios\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Create Scenario\</emph\> dialog appears.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3166426				0	lv	Enter a name for the new scenario and leave the other fields unchanged with their default values. Close the dialog with OK. Your new scenario is automatically activated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149664				0	lv	Using Scenarios				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3153415				0	lv	Scenarios can be selected in the Navigator:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3150752	12			0	lv	Open the Navigator with the \<emph\>Navigator\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id1593676\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_navigator.png\" width=\"0.1665inch\" height=\"0.1665inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id1593676\"\>Navigator icon\</alt\>\</image\> on the Standard bar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3155764	13			0	lv	Click the \<emph\>Scenarios\</emph\> icon \<image id=\"img_id7617114\" src=\"sc/res/imglst/navipi/na07.png\" width=\"0.222inch\" height=\"0.222inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id7617114\"\>Scenarios icon\</alt\>\</image\> in the Navigator.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3154256				0	lv	In the Navigator, you see the defined scenarios with the comments that were entered when the scenarios were created.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id1243629				0	lv	Double-click a scenario name in the Navigator to apply that scenario to the current sheet.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id9044770				0	lv	To delete a scenario, right-click the name in the Navigator and choose \<emph\>Delete\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3674123				0	lv	To edit a scenario, right-click the name in the Navigator and choose \<emph\>Properties\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3424481				0	lv	To hide the border of a set of cells that are part of a scenario, open the \<emph\>Properties\</emph\> dialog for each scenario that affects the cells and clear the Display border checkbox. Hiding the border also removes the listbox on the sheet where you can choose the scenarios.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3154368				0	lv	If you want to know which values in the scenario affect other values, choose \<emph\>Tools - Detective - Trace Dependents\</emph\>. You see arrows to the cells that are directly dependent on the current cell.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\scenario.xhp	0	help	par_id3154484				0	lv	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/06050000.xhp\" name=\"Creating Scenarios\"\>Creating Scenarios\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_updatetable.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Updating Pivot Tables				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_updatetable.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150792				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>pivot table import\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>pivot table function; refreshing tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>recalculating;pivot tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>updating;pivot tables\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_updatetable.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150792				0	lv	\<variable id=\"datapilot_updatetable\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/datapilot_updatetable.xhp\" name=\"Updating Pivot Tables\"\>Updating Pivot Tables\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_updatetable.xhp	0	help	par_id3154684				0	lv	If the data of the source sheet has been changed, $[officename] recalculates the pivot table. To recalculate the table, choose \<emph\>Data - Pivot Table - Refresh\</emph\>. Do the same after you have imported an Excel pivot table into $[officename] Calc.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Applying Goal Seek				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145068				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>goal seeking;example\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>equations in goal seek\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating;variables in equations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;calculating equations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>examples;goal seek\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145068				0	lv	\<variable id=\"goalseek\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/goalseek.xhp\" name=\"Applying Goal Seek\"\>Applying Goal Seek\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3145171				0	lv	With the help of Goal Seek you can calculate a value that, as part of a formula, leads to the result you specify for the formula. You thus define the formula with several fixed values and one variable value and the result of the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153966				0	lv	Goal Seek Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3150871				0	lv	To calculate annual interest (I), create a table with the values for the capital (C), number of years (n), and interest rate (i). The formula is:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3152596				0	lv	I = C * n* i				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335				0	lv	Let us assume that the interest rate \<item type=\"literal\"\>i\</item\> of 7.5% and the number of years \<item type=\"literal\"\>n\</item\> (1) will remain constant. However, you want to know how much the investment capital \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\> would have to be modified in order to attain a particular return \<item type=\"literal\"\>I\</item\>. For this example, calculate how much capital \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\> would be required if you want an annual return of $15,000.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3155960				0	lv	Enter each of the values for Capital \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\> (an arbitrary value like \<item type=\"literal\"\>$100,000\</item\>), number of years \<item type=\"literal\"\>n \</item\>(\<item type=\"literal\"\>1\</item\>), and interest rate \<item type=\"literal\"\>i\</item\> (\<item type=\"literal\"\>7.5%\</item\>) in one cell each. Enter the formula to calculate the interest \<item type=\"literal\"\>I\</item\> in another cell. Instead of \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\>, \<item type=\"literal\"\>n\</item\>, and \<item type=\"literal\"\>i\</item\> use the \<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/relativ_absolut_ref.xhp\"\>reference to the cell\</link\> with the corresponding value.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3147001				0	lv	Place the cursor in the cell containing the interest \<item type=\"literal\"\>I\</item\>, and choose \<emph\>Tools - Goal Seek\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Goal Seek\</emph\> dialog appears.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088				0	lv	The correct cell is already entered in the field \<emph\>Formula Cell\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3166426				0	lv	Place the cursor in the field \<emph\>Variable Cell\</emph\>. In the sheet, click in the cell that contains the value to be changed, in this example it is the cell with the capital value \<item type=\"literal\"\>C\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3150369				0	lv	Enter the expected result of the formula in the \<emph\>Target Value\</emph\> text box. In this example, the value is 15,000. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3146978				0	lv	A dialog appears informing you that the Goal Seek was successful. Click \<emph\>Yes\</emph\> to enter the result in the cell with the variable value.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\goalseek.xhp	0	help	par_id3149409				0	lv	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/06040000.xhp\" name=\"Goal Seek\"\>Goal Seek\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\edit_multitables.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Copying to Multiple Sheets				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\edit_multitables.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149456				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>copying;values, to multiple sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>pasting;values in multiple sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data;inserting in multiple sheets\</bookmark_value\> \<bookmark_value\>sheets; simultaneous multiple filling\</bookmark_value\> 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\edit_multitables.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149456	3			0	lv	\<variable id=\"edit_multitables\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/edit_multitables.xhp\" name=\"Copying to Multiple Sheets\"\>Copying to Multiple Sheets\</link\> \</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\edit_multitables.xhp	0	help	par_id3150868				0	lv	In $[officename] Calc, you can insert values, text or formulas that are simultaneously copied to other selected sheets of your document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\edit_multitables.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768				0	lv	Select all desired sheets by holding down the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>key and clicking the corresponding register tabs that are still gray at the bottom margin of the workspace. All selected register tabs are now white.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\edit_multitables.xhp	0	help	par_idN10614				0	lv	You can use Shift+\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Page Up or Page Down to select multiple sheets using the keyboard.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\edit_multitables.xhp	0	help	par_id3147435				0	lv	Now when you insert values, text or formulas into the active sheet, they will also appear in the identical positions in the other selected sheets. For example, data entered in cell A1 of the active sheet is automatically entered into cell A1 of any other seleted sheet.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Formatting Numbers With Decimals				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145367				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>numbers;formatting decimals\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formats; numbers in tables\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>tables; number formats\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>defaults; number formats in spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>decimal places;formatting numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formatting;numbers with decimals\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formatting;adding/deleting decimal places\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>number formats; adding/deleting decimal places in cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>deleting; decimal places\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>decimal places; adding/deleting\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145367				0	lv	\<variable id=\"format_value\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/format_value.xhp\" name=\"Formatting Numbers With Decimals\"\>Formatting Numbers With Decimals\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576				0	lv	Enter a number into the sheet, for example, 1234.5678. This number will be displayed in the default number format, with two decimal places. You will see 1234.57 when you confirm the entry. Only the display in the document will be rounded off; internally, the number retains all four decimal places after the decimal point.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3154012				0	lv	To format numbers with decimals:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3147394				0	lv	Set the cursor at the number and choose \<emph\>Format - Cells\</emph\> to start the \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3153157				0	lv	On the \<emph\>Numbers\</emph\> tab you will see a selection of predefined number formats. In the bottom right in the dialog you will see a preview of how your current number would look if you were to give it a particular format.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3155766				0	lv	\<image id=\"img_id3149021\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_numberformatincdecimals.png\" width=\"0.222in\" height=\"0.222in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3149021\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3149256				0	lv	If you only want to modify the number of the decimal places displayed, the easiest method is to use the \<emph\>Number Format: Add Decimal Place\</emph\> or \<emph\>Number Format: Delete Decimal Place\</emph\> icons on the Formatting Bar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Displaying Formulas or Values				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153195				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>formulas; displaying in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>values; displaying in tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables; displaying formulas/values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>results display vs. formulas display\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>displaying; formulas instead of results\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153195				0	lv	\<variable id=\"formula_value\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/formula_value.xhp\" name=\"Displaying Formulas or Values\"\>Displaying Formulas or Values\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3150010				0	lv	If you want to display the formulas in the cells, for example in the form =SUM(A1:B5), proceed as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3151116				0	lv	Choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - View\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120				0	lv	In the \<emph\>Display\</emph\> area mark the \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\> box. Click OK.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3147396				0	lv	If you want to view the calculation results instead of the formula, do not mark the Formulas box.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_value.xhp	0	help	par_id3153157				0	lv	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060100.xhp\" name=\"Spreadsheet - View\"\>%PRODUCTNAME Calc - View\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Defining Background Colors or Background Graphics				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149346				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>spreadsheets; backgrounds\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>backgrounds;cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>tables; backgrounds\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; backgrounds\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>rows, see also cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>columns, see also cells\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149346				0	lv	\<variable id=\"background\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/background.xhp\" name=\"Defining Background Colors or Background Graphics\"\>Defining Background Colors or Background Graphics\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	par_id9520249				0	lv	You can define a background color or use a graphic as a background for cell ranges in $[officename] Calc.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	hd_id3144760				0	lv	Applying a Background Color to a $[officename] Calc Spreadsheet				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	par_id3155429				0	lv	Select the cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	par_id3149260				0	lv	Choose \<emph\>Format - Cells\</emph\> (or \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> from the context menu).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	par_id3152938				0	lv	On the \<emph\>Background\</emph\> tab page, select the background color.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146974				0	lv	Graphics in the Background of Cells				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	par_id3155414				0	lv	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Picture - From File\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664				0	lv	Select the graphic and click \<emph\>Open\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	par_id3153575				0	lv	The graphic is inserted anchored to the current cell. You can move and scale the graphic as you want. In your context menu you can use the \<emph\>Arrange - To Background\</emph\> command to place this in the background. To select a graphic that has been placed in the background, use the \<switchinline select=\"appl\"\>\<caseinline select=\"CALC\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/02110000.xhp\" name=\"Navigator\"\>Navigator\</link\>\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Navigator\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	par_id51576				0	lv	\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/background.xhp\"\>Watermarks\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	par_id3156180				0	lv	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05030600.xhp\" name=\"Background tab page\"\>\<emph\>Background\</emph\> tab page\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\background.xhp	0	help	par_id7601245				0	lv	\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/format_table.xhp\"\>Formatting Spreadsheets\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Renaming Sheets				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150398				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>renaming;sheets\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>sheet tabs;renaming\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>tables;renaming\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>names; sheets\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150398				0	lv	\<variable id=\"rename_table\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/rename_table.xhp\" name=\"Renaming Sheets\"\>Renaming Sheets\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id3155131				0	lv	Click the name of the sheet that you want to change.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id3146976				0	lv	Open the context menu and choose the \<emph\>Rename Sheet\</emph\> command. A dialog box appears where you can enter a new name.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id3149260				0	lv	Enter a new name for the sheet and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id3149667				0	lv	Alternatively, hold down the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Option key\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Alt key\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> and click on any sheet name and enter the new name directly.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id0909200810502833				0	lv	Sheet names can contain almost any character. Some naming restrictions apply when you want to save the spreadsheet to Microsoft Excel format.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id090920081050283				0	lv	When saving to Microsoft Excel format, the following characters are not allowed in sheet names:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id090920081050281				0	lv	colon :				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id0909200810502897				0	lv	back slash \\				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id090920081050299				0	lv	forward slash /				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id0909200810502913				0	lv	question mark ?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id090920081050298				0	lv	asterisk *				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id0909200810502969				0	lv	left square bracket [				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id0909200810502910				0	lv	right square bracket ]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id0909200810502971				0	lv	single quote ' as the first or last character of the name				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id090920081050307				0	lv	In cell references, a sheet name has to be enclosed in single quotes ' if the name contains other characters than alphanumeric or underscore. A single quote contained within a name has to be escaped by doubling it (two single quotes). For example, you want to reference the cell A1 on a sheet with the following name:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id0909200810503071				0	lv	This year's sheet				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id0909200810503054				0	lv	The reference must be enclosed in single quotes, and the one single quote inside the name must be doubled:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id0909200810503069				0	lv	'This year''s sheet'.A1				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\rename_table.xhp	0	help	par_id3155444				0	lv	The name of a sheet is independent of the name of the spreadsheet. You enter the spreadsheet name when you save it for the first time as a file. The document can contain up to 256 individual sheets, which can have different names.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Calculating in Spreadsheets				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150791				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>spreadsheets; calculating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas; calculating\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150791				0	lv	\<variable id=\"calculate\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calculate.xhp\" name=\"Calculating in Spreadsheets\"\>Calculating in Spreadsheets\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120				0	lv	The following is an example of a calculation in $[officename] Calc.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951				0	lv	Click in a cell, and type a number				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN10656				0	lv	Press Enter.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1065D				0	lv	The cursor moves down to the next cell.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064				0	lv	Enter another number.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066F				0	lv	Press the Tab key.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN10676				0	lv	The cursor moves to the right into the next cell.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253				0	lv	Type in a formula, for example, \<item type=\"literal\"\>=A3 * A4 / 100.\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_idN1068B				0	lv	 Press Enter.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343				0	lv	The result of the formula appears in the cell. If you want, you can edit the formula in the input line of the Formula bar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calculate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155378				0	lv	When you edit a formula, the new result is calculated automatically.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Sorting Data 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150767				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>database ranges; sorting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>sorting; database ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>data;sorting in databases\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150767				0	lv	\<variable id=\"database_sort\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/database_sort.xhp\" name=\"Sorting Database Ranges\"\>Sorting Data\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	par_id3145751				0	lv	Click in a database range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	par_id121020081121549				0	lv	If you select a range of cells, only these cells will get sorted. If you just click one cell without selecting, then the whole database range will get sorted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	par_idN10635				0	lv	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Sort\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	par_id121020081121547				0	lv	The range of cells that will get sorted is shown in inverted colors.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	par_idN10645				0	lv	Select the sort options that you want.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	par_idN1063D				0	lv	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_sort.xhp	0	help	par_id1846980				0	lv	\<link href=\"https://wiki.openoffice.org/wiki/Documentation/How_Tos/Defining_a_Data_Range\"\>Wiki page about defining a data range\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Entering Matrix Formulas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153969				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>matrices; entering matrix formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas; matrix formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;matrix formulas\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153969				0	lv	\<variable id=\"matrixformula\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/matrixformula.xhp\" name=\"Entering Matrix Formulas\"\>Entering Matrix Formulas\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153144				0	lv	The following is an example of how you can enter a matrix formula, without going into the details of matrix functions.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153188				0	lv	Assume you have entered 10 numbers in Columns A and B (A1:A10 and B1:B10), and would like to calculate the sum of each row in Column C.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3154321				0	lv	Using the mouse, select the range C1:C10, in which the results are to be displayed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149260				0	lv	Press F2, or click in the input line of the Formula bar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3154944				0	lv	Enter an equal sign (=).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3145252				0	lv	Select the range A1:A10, which contains the first values for the sum formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3144767				0	lv	Press the (+) key from the numerical keypad.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3154018				0	lv	Select the numbers in the second column in cells B1:B10.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150716				0	lv	End the input with the matrix key combination: Shift+\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Enter.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\matrixformula.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640				0	lv	The matrix area is automatically protected against modifications, such as deleting rows or columns. It is, however, possible to edit any formatting, such as the cell background.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Entering Fractions 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155411				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>fractions; entering\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>numbers; entering fractions \</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;fractions\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155411				0	lv	\<variable id=\"fraction_enter\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/fraction_enter.xhp\" name=\"Entering Fractions \"\>Entering Fractions \</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153968				0	lv	You can enter a fractional number in a cell and use it for calculation:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3155133				0	lv	Enter "0 1/5" in a cell (without the quotation marks) and press the input key. In the input line above the spreadsheet you will see the value 0.2, which is used for the calculation.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3145750				0	lv	If you enter "0 1/2" AutoCorrect causes the three characters 1, / and 2 to be replaced by a single character. The same applies to 1/4 and 3/4. This replacement is defined in \<emph\>Tools - AutoCorrect Options - Options\</emph\> tab.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3145367	44			0	lv	If you want to see multi-digit fractions such as "1/10", you must change the cell format to the multi-digit fraction view. Open the context menu of the cell, and choose \<emph\>Format cells. \</emph\>Select "Fraction" from the \<emph\>Category\</emph\> field, and then select "-1234 10/81". You can then enter fractions such as 12/31 or 12/32 - the fractions are, however, automatically reduced, so that in the last example you would see 3/8.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Naming Cells				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147434				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>cells; defining names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>names; defining for cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>values; defining names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>constants definition\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>variables; defining names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell ranges; defining names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>defining;names for cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulas; defining names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>addressing; by defined names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell names; defining/addressing\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>references; by defined names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>allowed cell names\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>renaming;cells\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147434				0	lv	\<variable id=\"value_with_name\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/value_with_name.xhp\" name=\"Naming Cells\"\>Naming Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id4391918				0	lv	Allowed names				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id2129581				0	lv	Names in Calc can contain letters, numeric characters, and some special characters. Names must start with a letter or an underline character.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id1120029				0	lv	Allowed special characters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3362224				0	lv	underline (_) 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id4891506				0	lv	period (.) - allowed within a name, but not as first or last character				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id2816553				0	lv	blank ( ) - allowed within a name, but not as first or last character, and not for a cell range				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id328989				0	lv	Names must not be the same as cell references. For example, the name A1 is invalid because A1 is a cell reference to the top left cell.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id32898987				0	lv	Names must not start with the letter R followed by a number. See the ADDRESS function for more information.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id4769737				0	lv	Names for cell ranges must not include blanks. Blanks are allowed within names for single cells, sheets and documents.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id1226233				0	lv	Naming cells and formulas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id5489364				0	lv	A good way of making the references to cells and cell ranges in formulas legible is to give the ranges names. For example, you can name the range A1:B2 \<emph\>Start\</emph\>. You can then write a formula such as "=SUM(Start)". Even after you insert or delete rows or columns, $[officename] still correctly assigns the ranges identified by name. Range names must not contain any spaces.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id953398				0	lv	For example, it is much easier to read a formula for sales tax if you can write "= Amount * Tax_rate" instead of "= A5 * B12". In this case, you would name cell A5 "Amount" and cell B12 "Tax_rate."				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id4889675				0	lv	Use the \<emph\>Define Names\</emph\> dialog to define names for formulas or parts of formulas you need more often. In order to specify range names,				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3153954				0	lv	Select a cell or range of cells, then choose \<emph\>Insert - Names - Define\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Define Names\</emph\> dialog appears.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3156283				0	lv	Type the name of the selected area in the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> field. Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>. The newly defined name appears in the list below. Click OK to close the dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id5774101				0	lv	You can also name other cell ranges in this dialog by entering the name in the field and then selecting the respective cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3154942				0	lv	If you type the name in a formula, after the first few characters entered you will see the entire name as a tip.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3154510				0	lv	Press the Enter key in order to accept the name from the tip.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749				0	lv	If more than one name starts with the same characters, you can scroll through all the names using the Tab key.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3153711				0	lv	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04070100.xhp\" name=\"Insert - Names - Define\"\>Insert - Names - Define\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Filtering Cell Ranges 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153541				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>cell ranges;applying/removing filters\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>filtering;cell ranges/database ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>database ranges;applying/removing filters\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>removing;cell range filters\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153541				0	lv	\<variable id=\"database_filter\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/database_filter.xhp\" name=\"Filtering Cell Ranges\"\>Filtering Cell Ranges\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3145069				0	lv	You can use several filters to filter cell ranges in spreadsheets. A standard filter uses the options that you specify to filter the data. An AutoFilter filters data according to a specific value or string. An advanced filter uses filter criteria from specified cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN10682				0	lv	To Apply a Standard Filter to a Cell Range				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3150398				0	lv	Click in a cell range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN10693				0	lv	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Filter - Standard Filter\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3156422				0	lv	In the \<emph\>Standard Filter\</emph\> dialog, specify the filter options that you want.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A5				0	lv	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153143				0	lv	The records that match the filter options that you specified are shown.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153728				0	lv	To Apply an AutoFilter to a Cell Range				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3144764				0	lv	Click in a cell range or a database range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id9303872				0	lv	If you want to apply multiple AutoFilters to the same sheet, you must first define database ranges, then apply the AutoFilters to the database ranges.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3154944				0	lv	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Filter - AutoFilter\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN106DB				0	lv	An arrow button is added to the head of each column in the database range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153878				0	lv	Click the arrow button in the column that contains the value or string that you want to set as the filter criteria.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN10749				0	lv	Select the value or string that you want to use as the filter criteria.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN1074C				0	lv	The records that match the filter criteria that you selected are shown.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN106E8				0	lv	To Remove a Filter From a Cell Range				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN1075C				0	lv	Click in a filtered cell range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_idN106EC				0	lv	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Filter - Remove Filter\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_filter.xhp	0	help	par_id4525284				0	lv	\<link href=\"https://wiki.openoffice.org/wiki/Documentation/How_Tos/Defining_a_Data_Range\"\>Wiki page about defining a data range\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Handling of Empty Cells				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146799				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>empty cells;handling of\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	hd_id1502121				0	lv	\<variable id=\"empty_cells\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/05/empty_cells.xhp\"\>Handling of Empty Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id8266853				0	lv	In older versions of the software, empty cells were forced to numeric 0 in some contexts and to empty string in others, except in direct comparison where =A1=0 and =A1="" both resulted in TRUE if A1 was empty. Emptiness now is inherited until used, so both =VLOOKUP(...)=0 and =VLOOKUP(...)="" give TRUE if the lookup resulted in an empty cell being returned. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id2733542				0	lv	A simple reference to an empty cell is still displayed as numeric 0 but is not necessarily of type numeric anymore, so also comparisons with the referencing cell work as expected. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id4238715				0	lv	For the following examples, A1 contains a number, B1 is empty, C1 contains the reference to B1:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id8277230				0	lv	A1: 1 B1: <empty> C1: =B1 (displays 0)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id4086428				0	lv	=B1=0 => TRUE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id9024628				0	lv	=B1="" => TRUE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3067110				0	lv	=C1=0 => TRUE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id8841822				0	lv	=C1="" => TRUE (previously was FALSE)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id4077578				0	lv	=ISNUMBER(B1) => FALSE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id9094515				0	lv	=ISNUMBER(C1) => FALSE (previously was TRUE)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id396740				0	lv	=ISNUMBER(VLOOKUP(1;A1:C1;2)) => FALSE (B1)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3859675				0	lv	=ISNUMBER(VLOOKUP(1;A1:C1;3)) => FALSE (C1, previously was TRUE)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id402233				0	lv	=ISTEXT(B1) => FALSE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id1623889				0	lv	=ISTEXT(C1) => FALSE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id7781914				0	lv	=ISTEXT(VLOOKUP(1;A1:C1;2)) => FALSE (B1, previously was TRUE)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id300912				0	lv	=ISTEXT(VLOOKUP(1;A1:C1;3)) => FALSE (C1)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id9534592				0	lv	=ISBLANK(B1) => TRUE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id4969328				0	lv	=ISBLANK(C1) => FALSE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id9635914				0	lv	=ISBLANK(VLOOKUP(1;A1:C1;2)) => TRUE (B1, previously was FALSE)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id2476577				0	lv	=ISBLANK(VLOOKUP(1;A1:C1;3)) => FALSE (C1)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id4217047				0	lv	Note that Microsoft Excel behaves different and always returns a number as the result of a reference to an empty cell or a formula cell with the result of an empty cell. For example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id2629474				0	lv	A1: <empty>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id8069704				0	lv	B1: =A1 => displays 0, but is just a reference to an empty cell				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\05\empty_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id4524674				0	lv	=ISNUMBER(A1) => FALSE				20200411 15:09:53
