helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158446				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>MUNIT function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3158446				0	lv	MUNIT				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154121				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_EINHEITSMATRIX\"\>Returns the unitary square array of a certain size.\</ahelp\> The unitary array is a square array where the main diagonal elements equal 1 and all other array elements are equal to 0.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155123				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3156271				0	lv	MUNIT(Dimensions)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3159390				0	lv	\<emph\>Dimensions\</emph\> refers to the size of the array unit.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10C9B				0	lv	You can find a general introduction to Array functions at the top of this page.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156162				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150949				0	lv	Select a square range within the spreadsheet, for example, from A1 to E5.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151260				0	lv	Without deselecting the range, select the MUNIT function. Mark the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box. Enter the desired dimensions for the array unit, in this case \<item type=\"input\"\>5\</item\>, and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150403				0	lv	You can also enter the \<item type=\"input\"\>=Munit(5)\</item\> formula in the last cell of the selected range (E5), and press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Shift+Command+Enter\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Shift+Ctrl+Enter\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3156143				0	lv	You now see a unit array with a range of A1:E5.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10FA7				0	lv	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159084				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>FREQUENCY function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159084				0	lv	FREQUENCY				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145777				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_HAEUFIGKEIT\"\>Indicates the frequency distribution in a one-column-array.\</ahelp\> The function counts the number of values in the Data array that are within the values given by the Classes array.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153347				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155498				0	lv	FREQUENCY(Data; Classes)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154352				0	lv	\<emph\>Data\</emph\> represents the reference to the values to be counted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148402				0	lv	\<emph\>Classes\</emph\> represents the array of the limit values.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D71				0	lv	You can find a general introduction to Array functions at the top of this page.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148981				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155904				0	lv	In the following table, column A lists unsorted measurement values. Column B contains the upper limit you entered for the classes into which you want to divide the data in column A. According to the limit entered in B1, the FREQUENCY function returns the number of measured values less than or equal to 5. As the limit in B2 is 10, the FREQUENCY function returns the second result as the number of measured values that are greater than 5 and less than or equal to 10. The text you entered in B6, ">25", is only for reference purposes.				20200411 15:09:53
