helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149282				0	lv	The formula in cell B16 is =DCOUNT(A1:E10;0;A13:E14)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150962				0	lv	Database Function Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155837				0	lv	The following items are the parameter definitions for all database functions:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149453				0	lv	\<emph\>Database\</emph\> is the cell range defining the database.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3151272	86			0	lv	\<emph\>DatabaseField\</emph\> specifies the column where the function operates on after the search criteria of the first parameter is applied and the data rows are selected. It is not related to the search criteria itself. Use the number 0 to specify the whole data range. \<variable id=\"quotes\"\>To reference a column by means of the column header name, place quotation marks around the header name. \</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3147083				0	lv	\<emph\>SearchCriteria\</emph\> is the cell range containing search criteria. If you write several criteria in one row they are connected by AND. If you write the criteria in different rows they are connected by OR. Empty cells in the search criteria range will be ignored.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3151188				0	lv	Choose \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060500.xhp\" name=\"Spreadsheet - Calculate\"\>%PRODUCTNAME Calc - Calculate\</link\> to define how $[officename] Calc acts when searching for identical entries.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3882869				0	lv	See also the Wiki page about \<link href=\"https://wiki.openoffice.org/wiki/Documentation/How_Tos/Conditional_Counting_and_Summation\"\>Conditional Counting and Summation\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150882				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DCOUNT function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>counting rows;with numeric values\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150882				0	lv	DCOUNT				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156133				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBANZAHL\"\>DCOUNT counts the number of rows (records) in a database that match the specified search criteria and contain numerical values.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156099				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153218				0	lv	DCOUNT(Database; DatabaseField; SearchCriteria)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153273	187			0	lv	For the DatabaseField parameter you can enter a cell to specify the column, or enter the number 0 for the entire database. The parameter cannot be empty. \<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/01/04060101.xhp#quotes\"/\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154743				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153623				0	lv	In the example above (scroll up, please), we want to know how many children have to travel more than 600 meters to school. The result is to be stored in cell B16. Set the cursor in cell B16. Enter the formula \<item type=\"input\"\>=DCOUNT(A1:E10;0;A13:E14)\</item\> in B16. The \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\> helps you to input ranges.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149142				0	lv	\<emph\>Database\</emph\> is the range of data to be evaluated, including its headers: in this case A1:E10. \<emph\>DatabaseField\</emph\> specifies the column for the search criteria: in this case, the whole database. \<emph\>SearchCriteria\</emph\> is the range where you can enter the search parameters: in this case, A13:E14.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3145652				0	lv	To learn how many children in second grade are over 7 years of age, delete the entry >600 in cell D14 and enter \<item type=\"input\"\>2\</item\> in cell B14 under Grade, and enter \<item type=\"input\"\>>7\</item\> in cell C14 to the right. The result is 2. Two children are in second grade and over 7 years of age. As both criteria are in the same row, they are connected by AND.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156123				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DCOUNTA function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>records;counting in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>counting rows;with numeric or alphanumeric values\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156123				0	lv	DCOUNTA				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156110				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBANZAHL2\"\>DCOUNTA counts the number of rows (records) in a database that match the specified search conditions, and contain numeric or alphanumeric values.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3143228				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3146893				0	lv	DCOUNTA(Database; DatabaseField; SearchCriteria)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149751				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153982				0	lv	In the example above (scroll up, please), you can search for the number of children whose name starts with an E or a subsequent letter. Edit the formula in B16 to read \<item type=\"input\"\>=DCOUNTA(A1:E10;"Name";A13:E14)\</item\>. Delete the old search criteria and enter \<item type=\"input\"\>>=E\</item\> under Name in field A14. The result is 5. If you now delete all number values for Greta in row 8, the result changes to 4. Row 8 is no longer included in the count because it does not contain any values. The name Greta is text, not a value. Note that the DatabaseField parameter must point to a column that can contain values.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147256				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DGET function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>cell contents;searching in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>searching;cell contents in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147256				0	lv	DGET				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3152801				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBAUSZUG\"\>DGET returns the contents of the referenced cell in a database which matches the specified search criteria.\</ahelp\> In case of an error, the function returns either #VALUE! for no row found, or Err502 for more than one cell found.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159344				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154696				0	lv	DGET(Database; DatabaseField; SearchCriteria)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153909				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155388				0	lv	In the above example (scroll up, please), we want to determine what grade a child is in, whose name was entered in cell A14. The formula is entered in cell B16 and differs slightly from the earlier examples because only one column (one database field) can be entered for \<emph\>DatabaseField\</emph\>. Enter the following formula:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153096				0	lv	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DGET(A1:E10;"Grade";A13:E14)\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150524				0	lv	Enter the name \<item type=\"input\"\>Frank\</item\> in A14, and you see the result 2. Frank is in second grade. Enter \<item type=\"input\"\>"Age"\</item\> instead of "Grade" and you will get Frank's age.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148833				0	lv	Or enter the value \<item type=\"input\"\>11\</item\> in cell C14 only, and delete the other entries in this row. Edit the formula in B16 as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149912				0	lv	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DGET(A1:E10;"Name";A13:E14)\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148813				0	lv	Instead of the grade, the name is queried. The answer appears at once: Daniel is the only child aged 11.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149766				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DMAX function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>maximum values in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>searching;maximum values in columns\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149766				0	lv	DMAX				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154903				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBMAX\"\>DMAX returns the maximum content of a cell (field) in a database (all records) that matches the specified search conditions.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150771				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3159157				0	lv	DMAX(Database; DatabaseField; SearchCriteria)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145420				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148442				0	lv	To find out how much the heaviest child in each grade weighed in the above example (scroll up, please), enter the following formula in B16:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148804				0	lv	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DMAX(A1:E10;"Weight";A13:E14)\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150510				0	lv	Under Grade, enter \<item type=\"input\"\>1, 2, 3,\</item\> and so on, one after the other. After entering a grade number, the weight of the heaviest child in that grade appears.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159141				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DMIN function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>minimum values in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>searching;minimum values in columns\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159141				0	lv	DMIN				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154261				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBMIN\"\>DMIN returns the minimum content of a cell (field) in a database that matches the specified search criteria.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147238				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148479				0	lv	DMIN(Database; DatabaseField; SearchCriteria)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151050				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148925				0	lv	To find the shortest distance to school for the children in each grade in the above example (scroll up, please), enter the following formula in B16:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149161				0	lv	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DMIN(A1:E10;"Distance to School";A13:E14)\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148917				0	lv	In row 14, under Grade, enter \<item type=\"input\"\>1, 2, 3,\</item\> and so on, one after the other. The shortest distance to school for each grade appears.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154274				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DAVERAGE function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>averages; in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>calculating;averages in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154274				0	lv	DAVERAGE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3166453				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBMITTELWERT\"\>DAVERAGE returns the average of the values of all cells (fields) in all rows (database records) that match the specified search criteria.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146955				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150710				0	lv	DAVERAGE(Database; DatabaseField; SearchCriteria)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152943				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149104				0	lv	To find the average weight of all children of the same age in the above example (scroll up, please), enter the following formula in B16:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153688				0	lv	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DAVERAGE(A1:E10;"Weight";A13:E14)\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155587				0	lv	In row 14, under Age, enter \<item type=\"input\"\>7, 8, 9,\</item\> and so on, one after the other. The average weight of all children of the same age appears.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159269				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DPRODUCT function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>multiplying;cell contents in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159269				0	lv	DPRODUCT				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3152879				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBPRODUKT\"\>DPRODUCT multiplies all cells of a data range where the cell contents match the search criteria.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149966				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154854				0	lv	DPRODUCT(Database; DatabaseField; SearchCriteria)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149802				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148986				0	lv	With the birthday party example above (scroll up, please), there is no meaningful application of this function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148462				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DSTDEV function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>standard deviations in databases;based on a sample\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148462				0	lv	DSTDEV				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154605				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBSTDABW\"\>DSTDEV calculates the standard deviation of a population based on a sample, using the numbers in a database column that match the given conditions.\</ahelp\> The records are treated as a sample of data. That means that the children in the example represent a cross section of all children. Note that a representative result can not be obtained from a sample of less than one thousand.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149427				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148661				0	lv	DSTDEV(Database; DatabaseField; SearchCriteria)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153945				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149934				0	lv	To find the standard deviation of the weight for all children of the same age in the example (scroll up, please), enter the following formula in B16:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150630				0	lv	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DSTDEV(A1:E10;"Weight";A13:E14)\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153536				0	lv	In row 14, under Age, enter \<item type=\"input\"\>7, 8, 9,\</item\> and so on, one after the other. The result shown is the standard deviation of the weight of all children of this age.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150429				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DSTDEVP function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>standard deviations in databases;based on populations\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150429				0	lv	DSTDEVP				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3145598				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBSTDABWN\"\>DSTDEVP calculates the standard deviation of a population based on all cells of a data range which match the search criteria.\</ahelp\> The records from the example are treated as the whole population.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145307				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149484				0	lv	DSTDEVP(Database; DatabaseField; SearchCriteria)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153322				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155431				0	lv	To find the standard deviation of the weight for all children of the same age at Joe's birthday party (scroll up, please), enter the following formula in B16:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3148411				0	lv	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DSTDEVP(A1:E10;"Weight";A13:E14)\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3143271				0	lv	In row 14, under Age, enter \<item type=\"input\"\>7, 8, 9,\</item\> and so on, one after the other. The result is the standard deviation of the weight for all same-aged children whose weight was checked.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154794				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DSUM function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>calculating;sums in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>sums;cells in Calc databases\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154794				0	lv	DSUM				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3149591				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBSUMME\"\>DSUM returns the total of all cells in a database field in all rows (records) that match the specified search criteria.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146128				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150989				0	lv	DSUM(Database; DatabaseField; SearchCriteria)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159079				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3152766				0	lv	To find the length of the combined distance to school of all children at Joe's birthday party (scroll up, please) who are in second grade, enter the following formula in B16:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3151312				0	lv	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DSUM(A1:E10;"Distance to School";A13:E14)\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3150596				0	lv	Enter \<item type=\"input\"\>2\</item\> in row 14 under Grade. The sum (1950) of the distances to school of all the children who are in second grade is displayed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155614				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DVAR function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>variances;based on samples\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155614				0	lv	DVAR				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3154418				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBVARIANZ\"\>DVAR returns the variance of all cells of a database field in all records that match the specified search criteria.\</ahelp\> The records from the example are treated as a sample of data. A representative result cannot be obtained from a sample population of less than one thousand.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154825				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3156138				0	lv	DVAR(Database; DatabaseField; SearchCriteria)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151257				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153701				0	lv	To find the variance of the weight of all children of the same age of the above example (scroll up, please), enter the following formula in B16:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153676				0	lv	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DVAR(A1:E10;"Weight";A13:E14)\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153798				0	lv	In row 14, under Age, enter \<item type=\"input\"\>7, 8, 9,\</item\> and so on, one after the other. You will see as a result the variance of the weight values for all children of this age.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153880				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DVARP function\</bookmark_value\>         \<bookmark_value\>variances;based on populations\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153880				0	lv	DVARP				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3155119				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DBVARIANZEN\"\>DVARP calculates the variance of all cell values in a database field in all records that match the specified search criteria.\</ahelp\> The records are from the example are treated as an entire population.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145774				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3153776				0	lv	DVARP(Database; DatabaseField; SearchCriteria)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151110				0	lv	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3147099				0	lv	To find the variance of the weight for all children of the same age at Joe's birthday party (scroll up, please), enter the following formula in B16:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3147322				0	lv	\<item type=\"input\"\>=DVARP(A1:E10;"Weight";A13:E14)\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060101.xhp	0	help	par_id3146902				0	lv	In row 14, under Age, enter \<item type=\"input\"\>7, 8, 9,\</item\> and so on, one after the other. The variance of the weight values for all children of this age attending Joe's birthday party appears.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12040000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	Filter				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12040000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150767				0	lv	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12040000.xhp\" name=\"Filter\"\>Filter\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12040000.xhp	0	help	par_id3155131				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Shows commands to filter your data.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12040000.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119				0	lv	$[officename] automatically recognizes predefined database ranges.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12040000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153363				0	lv	The following filtering options are available:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12040000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153728				0	lv	\<link href=\"text/shared/02/12090000.xhp\" name=\"Standard filter\"\>Standard filter\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12040000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159153				0	lv	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12040300.xhp\" name=\"Advanced filter\"\>Advanced filter\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	DAYS 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151328				0	lv	\<bookmark_value\>DAYS function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151328				0	lv	\<variable id=\"days\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_days.xhp\"\>DAYS\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days.xhp	0	help	par_id3155139				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_TAGE\"\>Calculates the difference between two date values.\</ahelp\> The result returns the number of days between the two days.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155184				0	lv	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days.xhp	0	help	par_id3149578				0	lv	DAYS(Date2; Date1)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days.xhp	0	help	par_id3151376				0	lv	\<emph\>Date1\</emph\> is the start date, \<emph\>Date2\</emph\> is the end date. If \<emph\>Date2\</emph\> is an earlier date than \<emph\>Date1\</emph\> the result is a negative number.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151001				0	lv	Examples				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days.xhp	0	help	par_id3159101				0	lv	=DAYS("2010-01-01"; NOW()) returns the number of days from today until January 1, 2010.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_days.xhp	0	help	par_id3163720				0	lv	=DAYS("1990-10-10";"1980-10-10") returns 3652 days.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\05110000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	lv	AutoFormat				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\05110000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149666				0	lv	\<variable id=\"autoformat\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05110000.xhp\" name=\"AutoFormat\"\>AutoFormat\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\05110000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145367				0	lv	\<variable id=\"autoformattext\"\>\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Use this command to apply an AutoFormat to a selected sheet area or to define your own AutoFormats.\</ahelp\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\05110000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148455				0	lv	Format				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\05110000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799				0	lv	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:LISTBOX:RID_SCDLG_AUTOFORMAT:LB_FORMAT\"\>Choose a predefined AutoFormat to apply to a selected area in your sheet.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
